1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,290
Just imagine.

2
00:00:01,310 --> 00:00:03,077
You're 8,000 metres up,

3
00:00:03,097 --> 00:00:04,377
in the pitch dark of night,

4
00:00:04,397 --> 00:00:08,225
and a young porter lies on the snow,
fighting for his life.

5
00:00:08,245 --> 00:00:09,907
His cousin sits beside him,

6
00:00:09,927 --> 00:00:11,938
trying to hold him steady.

7
00:00:11,958 --> 00:00:14,833
Then a long line of climbers heading up
arrives.

8
00:00:14,853 --> 00:00:18,460
The cousin says someone's crampon spikes
struck Hassan's leg,

9
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:20,680
and a voice said: move aside, make way.

10
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:22,037
And the line kept moving.

11
00:00:22,057 --> 00:00:25,716
A few hours later, about a hundred people
reached this mountain's summit.

12
00:00:25,736 --> 00:00:28,107
Flags were waved, photos were taken,

13
00:00:28,127 --> 00:00:30,028
and a new world record was set.

14
00:00:30,048 --> 00:00:33,315
But the man who walked ahead of them all,
carrying the ropes,

15
00:00:33,335 --> 00:00:35,845
is not in a single one of those photos.

16
00:00:35,865 --> 00:00:37,507
This is his story.

17
00:00:37,527 --> 00:00:40,520
And of the people who stood by him on the
last night of his life

18
00:00:40,540 --> 00:00:42,530
and after it.

19
00:00:42,550 --> 00:00:45,423
A cousin, who stayed by his side to the end.

20
00:00:45,443 --> 00:00:47,653
A stranger, who stopped when the line kept
moving.

21
00:00:47,673 --> 00:00:51,350
And a few Pakistanis, who went back a year
later to bring him home.

22
00:00:51,370 --> 00:00:54,010
Come, let's take you back to that night.

23
00:00:54,030 --> 00:00:56,123
The young man's name was Muhammad Hassan,

24
00:00:56,143 --> 00:00:59,127
from Tisar, a small village in Shigar.

25
00:00:59,147 --> 00:01:02,280
In Tisar, most households live off one kind
of work:

26
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:06,573
carrying foreign climbers' loads on their
backs to the mountains.

27
00:01:06,593 --> 00:01:10,717
For years Hassan carried these loads from
Askoli to K2 Base Camp,

28
00:01:10,737 --> 00:01:12,690
walking for days at a time.

29
00:01:12,710 --> 00:01:16,503
The official daily wage for this labour is
still about 1,700 rupees.

30
00:01:16,523 --> 00:01:18,683
At home, his wife, three small children

31
00:01:18,703 --> 00:01:22,490
and his mother, who has a breathing illness,
waited on his earnings.

32
00:01:22,510 --> 00:01:25,000
His brothers say Hassan had just one dream:

33
00:01:25,020 --> 00:01:27,035
to earn enough before winter

34
00:01:27,055 --> 00:01:29,720
to move his family somewhere less cold.

35
00:01:29,740 --> 00:01:31,805
That's why, in the summer of 2023,

36
00:01:31,825 --> 00:01:35,550
for the first time in his life, he decided
to go above Base Camp,

37
00:01:35,570 --> 00:01:37,847
because the pay up there is far higher.

38
00:01:37,867 --> 00:01:41,570
A Russian expedition hired three Pakistani
porters to fix ropes,

39
00:01:41,590 --> 00:01:43,372
and Hassan was one of them.

40
00:01:43,392 --> 00:01:46,413
But few people know one side of this story.

41
00:01:46,433 --> 00:01:48,524
According to a cameraman on that expedition,

42
00:01:48,544 --> 00:01:53,215
at Camp 3 the leader ordered all the
Pakistani porters to turn back.

43
00:01:53,235 --> 00:01:55,660
They all went back, except Hassan.

44
00:01:55,680 --> 00:01:57,343
So what drove him on?

45
00:01:57,363 --> 00:02:00,210
Worry over his mother's medicine, a dream of
schooling for his children,

46
00:02:00,230 --> 00:02:04,423
or, after years of carrying others' loads
toward that summit,

47
00:02:04,443 --> 00:02:06,302
the longing to one day stand there himself?

48
00:02:06,322 --> 00:02:08,233
Perhaps all three.

49
00:02:08,253 --> 00:02:11,330
Then came the night of 26 to 27 July.

50
00:02:11,350 --> 00:02:14,427
Hassan was now at the most dangerous spot on
K2,

51
00:02:14,447 --> 00:02:15,880
known as the Bottleneck.

52
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:17,730
It's a narrow gully of ice,

53
00:02:17,750 --> 00:02:21,932
with a huge wall of ice always hanging right
above it.

54
00:02:21,952 --> 00:02:25,373
As he climbed with the ropes, the snow
suddenly gave way under his feet,

55
00:02:25,393 --> 00:02:27,537
and he slipped, left hanging upside down on
the rope.

56
00:02:27,557 --> 00:02:30,317
According to reports, his oxygen mask broke,

57
00:02:30,337 --> 00:02:32,840
and his stomach was left bare to the
freezing wind.

58
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:35,680
He had no down suit, and no proper gloves.

59
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:37,423
His cousin tried to pull him up,

60
00:02:37,443 --> 00:02:40,695
and Hassan kept trying to climb back up
himself,

61
00:02:40,715 --> 00:02:42,673
until his strength gave out.

62
00:02:42,693 --> 00:02:44,663
Some Nepali Sherpas were nearby,

63
00:02:44,683 --> 00:02:48,270
but the cousin says they had no pulley to
haul the weight.

64
00:02:48,290 --> 00:02:50,390
It was the last good day of the season.

65
00:02:50,410 --> 00:02:52,090
Anyone who didn't reach the summit that day

66
00:02:52,110 --> 00:02:55,257
would lose lakhs of rupees and months of
effort.

67
00:02:55,277 --> 00:02:56,800
So the line did not stop.

68
00:02:56,820 --> 00:02:59,703
That night, 30 to 80 people passed him.

69
00:02:59,723 --> 00:03:02,710
And then, in the dark, a stranger stopped.

70
00:03:02,730 --> 00:03:05,767
He was a Brazilian cameraman named Gabriel,

71
00:03:05,787 --> 00:03:09,330
with the team of Norwegian climber Kristin
Harila.

72
00:03:09,350 --> 00:03:10,693
Gabriel had a pulley,

73
00:03:10,713 --> 00:03:13,807
and he hauled Hassan up and laid him back on
the route.

74
00:03:13,827 --> 00:03:18,010
Harila says Gabriel stayed with Hassan for
about two and a half hours.

75
00:03:18,030 --> 00:03:20,043
He kept giving Hassan his own oxygen,

76
00:03:20,063 --> 00:03:21,643
gave him warm water,

77
00:03:21,663 --> 00:03:24,350
and tried to keep his body warm.

78
00:03:24,370 --> 00:03:27,065
But when Gabriel's own oxygen began to run
out,

79
00:03:27,085 --> 00:03:29,393
he too was forced to leave.

80
00:03:29,413 --> 00:03:31,448
That same morning, a few hundred metres
higher,

81
00:03:31,468 --> 00:03:35,132
Harila and her Nepali partner Tenjen Sherpa
reached the summit.

82
00:03:35,152 --> 00:03:39,113
They had climbed all 14 of the world's peaks
above 8,000 metres

83
00:03:39,133 --> 00:03:40,990
in just 92 days,

84
00:03:41,010 --> 00:03:43,410
the fastest anyone has ever done it.

85
00:03:43,430 --> 00:03:45,750
And below, in the morning's drone footage,

86
00:03:45,770 --> 00:03:48,630
Hassan can still be seen moving one leg.

87
00:03:48,650 --> 00:03:51,553
A Pakistani climber is rubbing his chest,

88
00:03:51,573 --> 00:03:54,243
while people keep passing in front of him
and behind him.

89
00:03:54,263 --> 00:03:57,418
According to his cousin, between 10 and 11
in the morning,

90
00:03:57,438 --> 00:04:00,400
Muhammad Hassan took his last breath on that
same route.

91
00:04:00,420 --> 00:04:04,480
Climbers coming down from the summit had to
pass the same spot again.

92
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:06,735
And the line kept moving.

93
00:04:06,755 --> 00:04:09,325
So, could Muhammad Hassan have been saved?

94
00:04:09,345 --> 00:04:13,483
People on the mountain that day give three
different answers.

95
00:04:13,503 --> 00:04:17,320
Austrian climber Wilhelm Steindl later
watched the drone footage.

96
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:20,057
He says three to five people working
together

97
00:04:20,077 --> 00:04:22,235
could have brought Hassan down to Camp 4,

98
00:04:22,255 --> 00:04:24,400
where there was oxygen and a tent.

99
00:04:24,420 --> 00:04:28,108
In his words, no real rescue attempt was
ever made.

100
00:04:28,128 --> 00:04:32,020
Harila says her team helped Hassan for about
an hour and a half.

101
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:34,353
She says an avalanche also came down nearby,

102
00:04:34,373 --> 00:04:37,610
and bringing Hassan down in that snow was
simply impossible.

103
00:04:37,630 --> 00:04:39,540
And the Russian expedition's cameraman says

104
00:04:39,560 --> 00:04:41,070
an attempt should have been made,

105
00:04:41,090 --> 00:04:44,500
but most people had neither the strength nor
the skill.

106
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:48,420
And every Sherpa guide was responsible for
their own client's safety.

107
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:50,770
Only Hassan's own company claimed

108
00:04:50,790 --> 00:04:52,498
that he died within moments.

109
00:04:52,518 --> 00:04:57,070
Yet almost everyone who was there says he
stayed alive for hours.

110
00:04:57,090 --> 00:04:59,570
Whose account do you think is true?

111
00:04:59,590 --> 00:05:01,523
Tell us what you think in the comments.

112
00:05:01,542 --> 00:05:03,527
But whatever the answer,

113
00:05:03,547 --> 00:05:05,725
Hassan did not come home that day.

114
00:05:05,745 --> 00:05:07,707
His body could not be brought down,

115
00:05:07,727 --> 00:05:11,930
and for a whole year he lay buried under the
snow in the Bottleneck.

116
00:05:11,950 --> 00:05:15,193
During that year, another son was born in
Hassan's home,

117
00:05:15,213 --> 00:05:19,398
a son who would never see his father's face.

118
00:05:19,418 --> 00:05:23,150
Meanwhile, in September 2023, the
government's inquiry report had come out,

119
00:05:23,170 --> 00:05:25,126
calling it an accidental death.

120
00:05:25,146 --> 00:05:28,395
It found the company had not given Hassan
proper equipment,

121
00:05:28,415 --> 00:05:31,605
had not checked his experience, and had not
insured him.

122
00:05:31,625 --> 00:05:34,730
So it recommended banning the company for
two years.

123
00:05:34,750 --> 00:05:36,700
But this 70-page report

124
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:40,907
did not even mention the dozens of people
who walked past Hassan.

125
00:05:40,927 --> 00:05:42,355
And what was this work worth?

126
00:05:42,375 --> 00:05:43,916
Back then, if a porter died on the job,

127
00:05:43,936 --> 00:05:46,843
the family got about 2 lakh rupees,

128
00:05:46,863 --> 00:05:48,838
but Hassan wasn't even insured.

129
00:05:48,858 --> 00:05:52,830
One helicopter rescue from the mountain
costs about 20,000 dollars,

130
00:05:52,850 --> 00:05:55,645
while the work that makes the world's
records possible

131
00:05:55,665 --> 00:05:58,180
pays just 1,700 rupees a day.

132
00:05:58,200 --> 00:06:01,388
The tourism minister at the time made the
family three promises:

133
00:06:01,408 --> 00:06:05,548
free schooling for the children, a job for
the widow, and treatment for his mother.

134
00:06:05,568 --> 00:06:08,860
Meanwhile, the same Steindl, who had turned
back that day,

135
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:10,933
travelled to Tisar to meet Hassan's widow,

136
00:06:10,953 --> 00:06:12,722
and started a fundraiser for the family.

137
00:06:12,742 --> 00:06:16,835
About 170,000 dollars came in from around
the world.

138
00:06:16,855 --> 00:06:19,147
But Hassan was still in the snow.

139
00:06:19,167 --> 00:06:21,777
For a year, the family kept asking the
government for help,

140
00:06:21,797 --> 00:06:26,024
and finally they reached out to Pakistani
climber Naila Kiani.

141
00:06:26,044 --> 00:06:29,603
Naila Kiani also found no funding for this
inside the country,

142
00:06:29,623 --> 00:06:32,198
and once again Steindl paid the cost.

143
00:06:32,218 --> 00:06:36,397
In July 2024, five Pakistani climbers from
Sadpara village

144
00:06:36,417 --> 00:06:37,890
climbed back up to that same Bottleneck.

145
00:06:37,910 --> 00:06:41,167
They dug through the frozen ice to free
their brother,

146
00:06:41,187 --> 00:06:44,550
and brought him down step by step on ropes.

147
00:06:44,960 --> 00:06:46,398
According to the Alpine Club of Pakistan,

148
00:06:46,418 --> 00:06:50,483
it was the first recovery of its kind from
that altitude on K2.

149
00:06:50,503 --> 00:06:54,293
A Pakistan Army helicopter flew the body
near his village free of charge,

150
00:06:54,313 --> 00:06:58,937
and in his village in Shigar, a large crowd
offered his funeral prayers.

151
00:06:58,957 --> 00:07:03,213
A year later, Muhammad Hassan finally came
home.

152
00:07:03,233 --> 00:07:04,316
And the promises?

153
00:07:04,336 --> 00:07:07,945
According to Naila Kiani, Hassan's children
now have a new roof,

154
00:07:07,965 --> 00:07:11,863
land of their own, and money saved in the
bank until they're adults.

155
00:07:11,883 --> 00:07:14,718
But all of it is thanks to a stranger.

156
00:07:14,738 --> 00:07:19,495
Even three years later, that any of the
government's three promises was kept,

157
00:07:19,515 --> 00:07:23,194
we found no proof in any report or on any
government website.

158
00:07:23,214 --> 00:07:24,683
The law has changed,

159
00:07:24,703 --> 00:07:29,027
and every porter must now be insured for 20
to 30 lakh rupees.

160
00:07:29,047 --> 00:07:33,080
But in 2025, another porter died on K2,

161
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:37,675
and the government's own advisory said his
claim was still being processed.

162
00:07:37,695 --> 00:07:40,930
To this day, we've found no report that it
was paid.

163
00:07:40,950 --> 00:07:44,780
Every year, countless photos come down from
K2's summit

164
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:46,468
of waving flags and new records.

165
00:07:46,488 --> 00:07:49,020
But you never see the people

166
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:51,567
who fix the ropes, carry the loads,

167
00:07:51,587 --> 00:07:53,880
and sometimes never come back.

168
00:07:53,900 --> 00:07:56,237
Their faces are never in the summit photo,

169
00:07:56,257 --> 00:07:58,060
and Muhammad Hassan's wasn't either.

170
00:07:58,080 --> 00:08:00,017
If you had been in that line that night,

171
00:08:00,037 --> 00:08:03,535
with the biggest dream of your life just a
few hundred metres away,

172
00:08:03,555 --> 00:08:04,555
would you have stopped?

173
00:08:04,575 --> 00:08:05,963
Tell us in the comments,

174
00:08:05,983 --> 00:08:09,776
and subscribe to Suraagh Nama for more
stories like this.
